{
 "schemaVersion": 1,
 "id": "kukhnya-s01e04-scene-19",
 "pair": "ru-en",
 "title": "Обед для персонала  (Staff Lunch)",
 "level": "A1 — Duolingo Section 2",
 "labels": {
  "max": "Макс",
  "senya": "Сеня"
 },
 "segments": [
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "The staff are hungry, and Max is in charge of lunch. Senya asks when it will be ready, and Max promises a special soup that is nothing like the usual slop. Here's the Russian first.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Макс, мы сегодня обедать будем?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Конечно, всё уже почти готово!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Мы все очень хотим есть.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Подождите ещё пару минут.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Что ты нам приготовил сегодня?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я сварил особенный суп-пюре.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Обычный суп был бы лучше.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Вы все пальчики оближете!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Надеюсь, это можно есть.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Вы такого никогда не пробовали.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Главное, чтобы было вкусно.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Это не то обычное месиво.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Ладно, неси скорее на стол!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Сейчас я вас всех удивлю!",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Now the wait for lunch.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Макс, мы сегодня обедать будем?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Max, are we going to have lunch today?' \"Obedat'\" is \"to have lunch\", from \"obed\". Russian puts the verb first, as \"have-lunch will we?\".",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Конечно, всё уже почти готово!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Of course, everything is already almost ready!' \"Pochti\" is \"almost\". \"Gotovo\" ends in -o to agree with \"vsyo\", everything.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Мы все очень хотим есть.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'We all really want to eat.' Russians say \"we want to eat\" for \"we're hungry\". \"Khotim\" is \"we want\".",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Подождите ещё пару минут.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Wait a couple more minutes.' \"Podozhdite\" is the polite or plural \"wait\". \"Paru\" is \"a couple\", and \"yeshchyo\" is \"more\".",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Что ты нам приготовил сегодня?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'What did you prepare for us today?' \"Nam\" is \"to us\", Russian's way to say \"for us\". \"Prigotovil\" is a man's \"prepared\".",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я сварил особенный суп-пюре.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I cooked a special purée soup.' \"Svaril\" has \"s-\", done, in front of \"varit'\", to boil. \"Sup-pyure\" is borrowed from French.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Обычный суп был бы лучше.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'A regular soup would be better.' \"By\" is the little word that makes \"would\": \"byl by luchshe\", \"would be better\".",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Вы все пальчики оближете!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'You will all lick your fingers!' \"Pal'chiki oblizhete\" is literally \"you'll lick the little fingers\", and it's the exact Russian for \"finger-licking\". \"Pal'chiki\" is \"little fingers\".",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Надеюсь, это можно есть.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I hope this is edible.' \"Mozhno\" is \"it's possible\", so \"mozhno yest'\" means \"can be eaten\". Senya is not convinced.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Вы такого никогда не пробовали.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'You have never tasted such a thing.' Max claims they have never tasted such a thing: \"never not tried\", the double negative, and \"such\" in its \"of\" form.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Главное, чтобы было вкусно.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'The main thing is that it is tasty.' \"Chtoby\" is \"so that\", and Russians put the next word in the past, \"bylo\", to express a hope. \"Vkusno\" is \"tasty\".",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Это не то обычное месиво.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'This is not that usual slop.' \"Mesivo\" is a rude word for a mush or slop, from \"mesit'\", to knead. \"Obychnoye\" is \"usual\".",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Ладно, неси скорее на стол!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Alright, bring it quickly to the table!' \"Nesi\" is \"carry\", the family of \"prinesu\". \"Na stol\" is \"onto the table\", with no ending for movement.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Сейчас я вас всех удивлю!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Now I will surprise you all!' \"Udivlyu\" is \"I will surprise\", and the root is \"udivitel'nyy\", surprising. \"Vas vsekh\" is \"all of you\". Now let's listen to the whole conversation again from start to finish.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Макс, мы сегодня обедать будем?",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Конечно, всё уже почти готово!",
   "gap": 0.5
